1) Typically the illness begins with pharyngtis
or tonsilitis accompanied by fever and malaise.
2) Whether of not specific antibiotic therapy
is given, respiratory symptoms and fever disappear
after a few days, and the patient feel entirely well.
3) One or two weeks after the onset of the illness,
weakness and anorexia return, and the patient suddenly
notices that his urine is scanty in amount and smoky in
appearance.
4) Upon awakening the next morning, he notes swelling
around the eyes and complains of shortness of breath
and headache.    (page 419)
5) After a few days, during which manifestations of the
disease remain intense, the signs and symptomes gradually
abate.
6) A diuresis begins, hematocrit and plasma protein
concentration rise, circulatory congestion disappears,
and blood urea nitrogen concentration falls.
7) Gross hematuria ceases, but proteinuria and microscopic
hematuria persist.
8) Hyaline, granular, and red blood cells casts are still
easily found, but within several weeks the patient feels
entirely well and is eager to return to his normal activities.
9) Six or seven months later the majority of such patients
no longer have proteinuria, but often the urine still contains
red blood cells and casts.
10) The antistreptolysin-O (ASO) titer, if elevated during
the acute episode, will in most cases have returned to normal.
11) At the end of a year microscopic hematuria and cylinduria
have usually disappeared.
12) As mentioned earlier, in some instances the illness has a 
more insidious character.  A week of two after a respiratory
infection the patient notes slight ankle edema, and examination
of the urine reveals protein, red blood cells, and casts.
13) After a brief period edema disappears, and the patient
feels entirely well.    (page 420)
14) On the basis of these findings it is now possible to
account for the striking seasonal differences in the peak 
incidence of the disease in various geographical regions.
In the North the peak incidence is in the winter and spring,
corresponding to the period when respiratory infections are
most common, whereas in the South the majority of cases occur
in the summer, the time at which skin infections are most
prevalent.
15) The ASO titer ordinarily begins to rise one to three
weeks after a hemolytic streptococcal infection and reaches
a maximum (as high as several thousand units) in three to
five weeks.
16) Subsequently, there is a gradual decline, but the rate
of fall is quite variable.
17) In about 50 per cent of the patients the titer returns
to normal within six months and in 75 per cent within one
year.
18) In a few cases abnormal titers persist for as long as two 
years.
19) However, by the use of bacteriologic studies during the
acute attack and ASO determinations both during and after
the acute illness, evidence of streptococcal infection can
be obtained in almost all cases.    (page 422)
20) Antibodies to the M protein develop slowly, some
patients first showing an elavated titer as late as two
months after the infection.
21) Demonstrable type-specific antibody often disappears
from the bloodstream within a few months, but it may persist
for many years after the initiating infection has occured.
22) It seems, moreover, that type-specific immunity outlasts
the presence of measurable antibody in the bloodstream;
23) This view {that only one organism is responsible for
the streptococcal infection} is supported by the observation
that only one of the two elevated titers tends to persist
over a prolonged period, presumably indicating the type of the
infecting organism.
24) For example, in patients given 250,000 units of penicillin
twice daily parenterally for 10 days bacteriostatic antibodies
do not develop;
25) Compliment levels usually return to normal within a month
after the acute illness.      (page 423)
26) The persistence of a low serum complement level (presumably
giving evidence of continued activity of the basic immunologic
process) has also been said to augur a poor prognosis, but this
correlation has not been observed by other investigators.
27) Between the onset of a streptococcal infection and the 
development of symptoms or signs of acute glomerulonephritis,
there is a latent period that usually ranges between one and two
weeks and that averages 10 days.  
28) Latent periods shorter than a week are not uncommon,
however, and occur in as many as one-fifth of the cases.
29) Only in an occasional patient does acute glomerulonephritis
develop as ling as three to four weeks after the inciting 
infection.
30) Latent periods of more than one month have not been noted,
and it is doubtful whether an infection that precedes the onset
of renal abnormalities by such a period has a relation to
subsequent renal disease.
31) After the acute infection has subsided, microscopic 
hematuria and proteinuria usually disappear, only to recur
at the onset of the acute attack one or two weeks later.
32) It is of interest that acute exacerbations of chronic 
glomerulonephritis ordinarily occur after a much shorter 
latent period than a first attack, usually within one to four
days after the onset of a streptococcal infection.
33) However, exacerbations occasionally occur after an interval
of one to two weeks, and for this reason the length of the
latent period cannot in itself be taken as an absolute criterion
for differentiating between an exacerbation and an initial attack.
			(page 424)
34) These symptoms usually disappear during the initial period
of bed rest.
35) In an occasional patient the outstanding symptom is marked
weakness, which may persist for several months after the acute 
attack.
36) High fever and chills do not occur commonly during the
acute phase and when present usually indicate that the initial
streptococcal infection is still present.
37) In most cases, gross hematuria disappears after a few days,
but it may continue for one or two weeks.
38) Microscopic hematuria can, of course, be found for a much
longer period and often persists even after significant 
proteinuria is no longer present.
39) In some cases, hematuria has been observed for as long as
several years after all other abnormalities have vanished.
			(page 425)
40) This view is borne out by the clinical observation that
facial edema is most prominent on awakening and tends to 
disappear after the patient has been upright for a few hours.
41) However, it {hypertension} may sometimes be overlooked
because it is present only transiently, or because "normal"
pressures recorded during the illness were noth compared with
readings taken after recovery.    (page 426)
42) As diuresis occurs, the cardiac silhouette returns to
normal, usually within several days.
43) In a few cases, the heart remains enlarged for as long
as several months after the acute attack.  (page 427)
44) The signs and symptoms of circulatory congestion continue
unabated {despite digitalis treatment} until the usual diuresis
occurs, and hydremia is relieved.
45) In recent years there has been a decrease in the frequency
of this complication {hypertensive encephalopathy}, probably
because severe hypertension, when it occurs, usually is promptly
controlled by means of the newer antihypertensive drugs.
			(page 429)
46) Urgency and dysuria are rare in uncomplicated acute
glomerulonephritis; when these symptoms occur, the presence
of a superimposed urinary tract infection should be suspected.
47) Most patients with purpura and nephritis do not have
evidence of a preceding streptococcal infection, and frequently
they have signs of some other disease...
48) Acute rheumatic fever rarely occurs in association with
acute glomerulnephritis, and it seems the the coexistance of
the two diseases is fortuitous.		(page 430)
49) During recovery from acute glomerulnephritis, there is a 
progressive rise in both glomerular filtration rate and 
filtration fraction.
50) In most patients who are destined to heal, this improvement
occurs within the first several weeks after the onset of 
symptoms, but in an occasional case a mild disturbance in renal
function may persist for a longer time.   (page 431)
51) Fatty elements, including oval fat droplets, may also be
seen form time to time but are usually present only in small
numbers.
52) The duration of proteinuria is extremely variable. 
53) In some patients protein excretion returns to normal levels
within a matter of days, whereas in others, in whom healing
eventually takes place, it persists for weeks or months.
			(page 432)
54) Microscopic hematuria usually outlasts proteinuria by
several weeks or months and may sometimes be present for as long
as several years after the latter has disappeared.
55) The urine of patients with persistent microscopic hematuria
usually contains a few casts, including red blood cell casts, 
and these too may persist for prolonged periods.
56) A sudden transient increase in the degree of hematuria and
proteinuria may occur in the convalescent patient in association
with a new streptococcal infection, even when the infecting
organism is not nephritogenic in character.
57) The urine should be examined within 20 or 30 minutes after
it is voided, because casts and, to a lesser degree, red blood
cells are apt to deteriorate on prolonged standing. (page 433)
58) As diuresis occurs (and plasma concentration contracts),
both hemoglobin and hematocrit return to normal levels.
59) There is nearly always an increase in ertythrocyte 
sedimentation rate in the early phase of the disease.
60) As the acute attack subsides, the sedimentation rate
ordinarily returns slowly to normal.
61) In most patients this change more or less parallels the
healing of the renal lesion.
62) In some, however, the sedimentation rate returns to 
normal before full recovery has taken place, and in others
it remains elevated even after all clinical evidence of renal
disease has disappeared.    (page 434)
63) Elevations in plasma concentrations are seen as early
as two to four days after the onset of the illness and usually
disappear within three to four weeks.
64) Severe abnormalities such as massive glomerular 
proliferation and extensive thrombosis and fibriniod necrosis
have an ominous prognostic significance; in most cases these
lesions lead to oliguria, progressive renal failure, and death
from uremia within a short time.   (page 437)
65) Death typically occurs within several months of the onset
of the illness. {Note: this is out of context}  (page 438)
66) Studies early this century suggested that diffuse 
glomerular lesion rarely occurred in patients with positive
blood cultures but instead was restricted almost exclusively
to those in the "abacterial phase".
67) Subsequent observations (in the pre-penicillin era)
did not confirm these findings.
68) Early workers ascribed them {focal lesions of focal
embolic glomerulonephritis} to bacterial emboli originating
from the heart valves, but more recently it has been 
suggested that they are the result of an immunologic disorder
similar to that proposed for the renal lesion of the acute
glomerulonephritis.		
69) In the preantibiotic era uremia contributed to the fatal
outcome in about 5 to 10 per cent of cases.   (page 439)
70) In patients dying despite therapy, diffuse and focal
embolic lesions are seen with about the same frequency as in
untreated patients studied in the prepenicillin era.
71) Slight impairment of the renal function is still 
encountered commonly, but if not present before the beginning
of therapy, it is unlikely to appear later.
72) After appropriate antibiotic therapy, function usually
returns to normal.
73) Proteinuria and sediment abnormalities, which are 
frequently seen even in patients without renal dysfunction,
ordinarily diminish or disappear during the course of 
treatment; clinical evidence of renal disease is thus rarely
manifest for more than a few months.
74) On the other hand, residual histologic changes often
persist indefinitely.
75) Until recent years, the mortality rate in the acute phase 
of glomerulnephritis was approximately 5 to 10 per cent.
76) In the last decade or two, this pattern has changed.
			(page 440)
77) Although anuria of more than three or four days' duration
usually presages a fatal outcome, such a result does not always
follow.
78) An occasional patient undergoes a diuresis and demonstrates
considerable improvement of renal function even after periods
of anuria ranging up to four or five weeks.
79) Progression into the subacute form of the disease is an 
ominous prognostic sign because it is charaterized by relentless
deterioration of renal function, with death usually occurring
in a period of six months to two years.   (page 441)
80) Observations in the preantibiotic era suggested that
persistance of the streptococcal infection during an attack
of acute glomerulnephritis was accompanied by increased
tendency to chronic renal disease, but this view has been
disputed.
81) In a matter of four to six months after the acute attack
the urines of approximately half the adults and the great
majority of children are free of protein.
82) Within 12 months most patients destined to heal have
already done so; if there is a continued abnormal excretion
of protein beyond this time, there are reasonable grounds for
concern that the patient has entered the latent phase.
83) However, complete recovery sometimes takes place even
when the proteinuria has persisted for longer than a year.
84) As mentioned earlier, microscopic hematuria and red-cell
casts may be found for as long as several years after 
proteinuria has disappeared;
85) There is little evidence that hypertension occurs as a
sequel to healed acute glomerulnephritis; long-term studies
of patients who have recovered from acute glomerulnephritis
indicate that persistent elevation of blood pressure is a
uncommon finding.
86) The only evidence favoring the existence of "postnephrotic
hypertension" is derived from the observations on several
patients in whom an elevated blood pressure persisted for as 
long as one or two years after proteinuria had disappeared. 
87) Patients who do not heal and enter so-called latent stage
of glomerulnephritis often remain well for many years.
88) Eventually, progressive renal insufficiency slowly develops
in many of these patients, but a few retain normal renal
function for several decades or longer and ultimately succumb
to an unrelated illness.       (page 442)
89) As mentioned earlier, the nephrotic syndrome may also
appear several weeks after the onset of the acute attack,
heralding the development of subacute glomerulnephritis.
90) In an occasional patient, the nephrotic syndrome develops
long after the acute attack, in the course of the latent phase
of the disease.
91) Many years ago it was observed that patients who have
fully recovered from an attack of acute glomerulnephritis rarely
suffer a second attack even if they contract a subsequent 
infection with beta-hemolytic streptococci.
92) When penicillin is administered early in the course of a
streptococcal infection, the type-specific antibody response
is inhibited, and therefore the usual immunity to reinfection
cannot be relied upon.
93) Whether or not second attacks are occuring more often now
than in the pre-antibiotic era is not possible to say, but 
future observations on the natural history of the disease should
serve to answer this question.		(page 443)
94) As the first step in diagnosis, it should be determined
whether the patient has suffer earlier from an illness suggestive
of acute glomerulnephritis or if proteinuria has been found on
previous examinations.
95) Another feature of the history that may be of help is the 
duration of the latent period.
96) If only one or two days have elapsed from the onset of the 
streptococcal infection, there is a strong likelihood that the 
attack represents an acute recurrence in a patient with chronic
disease.
97) When the history is inconclusive, several physical and
laboratory findings may be of diagnostic value.
98) In most cases {where acute tubular necrosis presents a 
clinical picture difficult to distinguish from that seen in the
oliguric patient with acute glomerulnephritis}, however, the 
history will provide valuable diagnostic information.
99) In acute tubular necrosis there will usually be an obvious
predisposing cause such as prolonged hypotension, trauma,
a transfusion reaction, or exposure to nephrotoxic agents.  
			(page 444)
100) Patients with focal glomerulnephritis commonly give a
history of gross hematuria that has developed closely on the
heels of a respiratory infection.
101) Long-term observations will usually provide further help
in clarify the problem. 
102) Patients who have had poststreptococcal nephritis and 
have either recovered or entered the latent phase rarely have
recurrent bouts of gross hematuria, whereas those with focal
nephritis commonly have such recurrences.
103) In addition, patients with hereditary nephritis frequently
have a positive family history of nerve deafness and of death
from renal failure.
104) Furthermore, the course of these disorders {other 
glomerulitides} is often stormy with progressive renal failure
developing within a few weeks or months.   (page 445)
105) Patients with idiopathic nephrotic syndrome ordinarily
do not have a previous history of respiratory infection....
106) In patients with the idiopathic nephrotic syndrome,
hypoalbuminemia, hypercholersterolemia, massive proteinuria,
and edema ordinarily persist for many months.
107) In the occasional patient in whom a nephrotic picture
occurs as a feature of acute glomerulnephritis, the nephrotic 
syndrome is usually of short duration, typically lasting only
for a few days or weeks.
108) Furthermore, extreme elavations of blood pressure,
even when they occur in acute glomerulnephritis, are usually
too brief to produce the retinal hemorrhages, exudates, and 
papilledema characteristic of malignant hypertension.
109) A reduction in kidney size also indicates that the
hypertension is relatively long standing and is almost certainly
not the result of acute nephritis.  (page 446)
110) These changes {proteinuria, hematuria, and cylinduria}
occur not only after contact sports such as football and boxing,
in which there is direct trauma to the kidneys, but also after
other vigorous activities such as swimming and rowing.
111) The abnormalities usually disappear within one or two days
and have no clinical significance.
112) The explanation for the urinary findings is readily 
arrived at by means of a carefully taken history and by 
reexaminations of the urine after the patient has avoided
exercise for several days.j
113) Mild proteinuria and cylinduria occur commonly at the 
height of a variety of acute febrile illnesses.
114) Acute glomerulnephritis often follows in the wake of a
streptococcal infection even when the organism has been 
eradicated promptly by appropriate antibiotic therapy.  
			(page 447)
115) Reserpine, administered intramuscularly or intravenously,
will nearly always induce a marked reduction in blood pressure
within one or two hours.    (page 448)
116) Further quanities of the drug should be given at intervals
of several hours as required.
117) Mild side effects such as somnolence, nasal congestion,
and weakness can be anticipated, but since treatment is usually
necessary for only a few days, these symptoms rarely become a
significant problem.
118) More serious symptoms of reserpine toxicity, including
depression, bizarre dreams, and the parkinsonian syndrome,
are not likely to occur unless large doses of the compound
are given for a long time.     (page 449)
119) On this basis, it has often been recomended that if
proteinuria and sediment abnormalities persist, the patient
should be kept in bed for as long as 6 to 12 months.
120) As a general rule, it seems that one should recommend
bed rest only for as long as there is evidence of continued
improvement of renal function or of a striking and progressive
reduction in the excretion of protein and formed elements.
121) If, however, a marked increase in protein and red-cell
excretion follows closely upon increased activity, the patient
should probably return to bed for an additional period of 
several weeks, after which ambulation can again be attemped.
			(page 450)
122) Such exacerbations {hematuria, cylinduria and proteinuria
following a tonsillectomy} usually last for only a few days
but may persist in some cases for as long as one or two weeks.
123) Treatment {of a urinary tract infection} should be
continued for three to four weeks.    (page 451)


